            "text": "Fills a tensor with random whole numbers from the GEOMETRIC distribution:\nhow many tries it takes to get the first success, keeping the tensor's shape.\n\nEach element becomes the number of the try on which a coin with success chance p first comes up: 1 (success at once) is the most likely, 2 next, and so on, each count (1 - p) times as likely as the one before. It is the whole-number cousin of the exponential wait. Use it for random run lengths, repeat counts, or how many steps before a change. There is no t.dist.geometric.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.geometric\nThe node takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to fill. Only its SHAPE, dtype and device are used - its values are ignored, and it is not changed (the node fills a copy). A list or number is turned into a tensor first. Every tensor that arrives is filled and sent; this is the only inlet that makes the node send. Unlike the other fills it also works on an integer tensor, and the counts then come out as integers; on a float tensor they are floats (1.0, 2.0 ...), always whole.\n\np (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe chance of success on each try, kept by the widget between 0.000001 and 0.999999. The average count is 1 / p. Default 0.5.\n\nThe parameters are drag floats on the node and also inlets. They are read when the next tensor arrives; setting one does not send. In the demo each slider goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the parameter first and THEN clicks the t.zeros, so a fresh tensor arrives and is filled at once.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nA new tensor, the same shape, dtype and device as the input, filled with fresh independent samples.\n\nTHE HISTOGRAM:\nt.histc divides the range from its min to its max into equal bins and counts how many of the 4096 samples land in each; the bar plot draws the counts, the first bin at the left. Its shape is the shape of the distribution. Samples outside min..max are not counted at all.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang clicks t.zeros once when the page opens, and its 64 x 64 tensor of zeros comes back from the node as 4096 samples; the zeros themselves never show, only their shape matters. The heat_map (1 dark, 8 bright) shows the counts as stepped tones. The histogram has one bar per count, 1 at the left to 20 at the right, each bar a fixed fraction of the one before: at p 0.5 half the samples are 1, a quarter 2, an eighth 3. Drag p down and the bars spread to the right; up towards 1 and nearly everything is 1.\n\nRELATED:\nt.exponential is the continuous version. t.dist.poisson and t.poisson are the other whole-number counts. t.dist.bernoulli is a single try."
        },
